# Paths whose review is reserved to a specific role, rather than any reviewer.
#
# GitHub requires the "Require review from Code Owners" branch-protection rule for
# these entries to be enforced; without it they only auto-request the reviewer.
#
# The owner below is a placeholder. Point every rule at your fork's owning role with:
#
#   make setup-replace-codeowners OWNERS='@example-org/tech-leads'

# Supply-chain controls. The npm `min-release-age` cooldown in each .npmrc is
# evaluated only while npm resolves dependencies, so a lockfile produced with it
# switched off installs cleanly under `npm ci` and shows no symptom in CI.
# Overriding the cooldown is a deliberate call — reacting to a CRITICAL advisory is
# a legitimate one — and this entry is what reserves that call to the owning role.
# `npm-cooldown-audit.yaml` detects and reports a bypass but never blocks it; this
# is the half that enforces.
#
# pnpm states the same policy in pnpm-workspace.yaml (minimumReleaseAge and the
# strictness flags around it, plus allowBuilds, blockExoticSubdeps and trustPolicy).
# It refuses at resolution time rather than warning afterwards, so there is no
# audit tool watching that half — the settings file itself is the control, which
# makes review of it the only thing standing between a weakened window and a
# lockfile nobody questions.
#
# The two bypass ledgers state the override differently again. The entries above
# are settings and lockfiles where a weakened window has to be inferred from what
# the file no longer refuses; a ledger is the override written out in full, one
# dated entry per module or tool taken early. That makes them the easiest of these
# to read and the easiest to add a line to, which is why the reservation matters
# here rather than mattering less.
**/.npmrc                             @Tomy-ch
**/package-lock.json                  @Tomy-ch
**/pnpm-lock.yaml                     @Tomy-ch
**/pnpm-workspace.yaml                @Tomy-ch
.github/actions-pin.toml              @Tomy-ch
.github/go-cooldown-bypass.toml       @Tomy-ch
.github/tool-cooldown-bypass.toml     @Tomy-ch
docker/images-pin.toml                @Tomy-ch

# The Go toolchain still has no cooldown of its own — nothing in `go get` refuses
# a freshly published version — so the refusal had to be built here, and taking one
# early is written down in the ledger above. The gate reads the `go.mod` diff and
# covers direct requirements only, which leaves indirect modules controlled by
# review alone and makes the entries below load-bearing rather than symmetrical
# bookkeeping.
go.mod                                @Tomy-ch
go.sum                                @Tomy-ch

# The version SSOT. `tool-cooldown.yaml` gates the CLI tools declared here, and taking
# one early is written down in the ledger above — but the file states its own exemption:
# the language runtimes are outside that check, on the ground that a poisoned runtime is
# not a supply-chain link a cooldown can protect. Those lines are therefore held by
# review alone, and `make sync-versions` carries them into go.mod, the Dockerfile FROM
# tags and the READMEs, so what is reviewed here is what every build then runs.
mise.toml                             @Tomy-ch

# The runner egress allowlist. Every scanning job runs `harden-runner` with
# egress-policy: block, so a host is reachable only by being named here, and
# `make egress-apply` writes the entry out to every workflow that carries the job.
# Widening it is the decision worth reserving: a host added here is one a compromised
# action can reach from a runner that holds the job's secrets, and the widening is a
# line in a list rather than anything that looks like a policy change.
# `make egress-check` holds the other half — it fails when a workflow and this file
# disagree, and says nothing about whether an entry belongs at all.
.github/egress.toml                   @Tomy-ch
